*.key

# Local data
*.db
*.sqlite3

# Vendored third-party source documents. Research cites and links sources; it does
# not redistribute them. A CONFIDENTIAL vendor PDF and an unlicensed RAML reached
# two public remotes on this project already - see docs/adr/ and the section 0
# licensing note in the research reports.
#
# WIDENED FROM docs/research/ TO THE WHOLE REPOSITORY (U15). Measured with
# `git check-ignore` before the change: `docs/research/test.pdf` was blocked
# while `test.pdf`, `docs/test.pdf`, `src/test.pdf` and `vendor.raml` were all
# stageable. The incident's mechanism was a broad `git add` sweeping files
# nobody intended to stage, which has happened twice here, and THE REPOSITORY
# ROOT - where such a sweep picks a file up - was exactly where the old
# path-scoped block did not apply.
#
# WHAT THIS COSTS, stated rather than assumed: a legitimate PDF someone later
# wants in the repository is now blocked too. There is no such file today - no
# tracked file matches any pattern below, checked with `git ls-files`, so this
# widening changes the status of nothing currently committed. When one is
# genuinely wanted the answer is `git add -f` PLUS an entry in
# `.file-type-allowlist`, because scripts/check-committed-file-types.py
# denylists these extensions independently and will still refuse it.
#
# THIS IS NOT THE CONTROL. `.gitignore` is bypassed by `git add -f` and does
# nothing about a file that is already tracked. It removes the accident, not
# the intent. The gate is the control; C8-I1 names both.
*.pdf
*.raml
*.doc
*.docx
*.ppt
*.pptx
*.xls
*.xlsx
*.zip

# Credential material the standard names explicitly
*.pem
secrets/
